The Art of Sculpture: From Marble to Modern Materials\par
\par
Sculpture is one of the oldest forms of human artistic expression. From prehistoric figurines to contemporary installations, sculptors have explored the third dimension using a wide range of materials and techniques. The choice of material has always shaped both the form and the meaning of the work.\par
\par
Marble was the preferred material of classical antiquity and the Renaissance. Its translucency and fine grain allowed sculptors to create lifelike human figures with delicate surfaces. Michelangelo famously said that every block of stone contains a statue and that the sculptor's task is simply to remove the excess. Bronze, on the other hand, allowed thinner and more dynamic forms. The lost wax technique made it possible to cast large equestrian monuments and intricate figure groups.\par
\par
In the twentieth century, sculptors began to experiment with new materials. Auguste Rodin introduced expressive, unfinished surfaces that emphasized the process of creation. Constantin Brancusi simplified forms to their geometric essence. Henry Moore explored the relationship between solid mass and empty space. Modern sculptors work with steel, glass, plastic, and even light, extending the boundaries of the discipline.\par
\par
Public sculpture plays a special role in urban life. Monuments commemorate historical events and figures, while abstract works can shape the identity of a city square or park. Debates over public monuments often reflect broader social and political tensions. Who should be remembered, and in what form, remains a question that every generation must answer for itself.\par
